Mark XXXI (part 2): GA hardening - licensing, updates, payments, hygiene

The ship-blocking gate. Every item adds a control with its test.

- Licensing hardening: payload fields device_id (key bound to a device
  fingerprint), max_devices (activation-time device counting, honest
  per-machine scope documented), exp (already present), and a revocation
  list (revoked.txt holding sha256 digests or full keys, checked at
  validation AND runtime). jarvis license trial: a local 7-day PRO trial
  with fingerprint reset-detection - documented as local-trust.
- Signed auto-update: jarvis update apply downloads the wheel named in
  the verified manifest (wheel_url + sha256 payload fields), checks the
  hash before install, stages, and installs via the audited argv runner;
  the previous version is recorded for jarvis update rollback.
  Signature/hash failures refuse the install.
- Payment queue: jarvis payhub serve (webhook receiver, HMAC-SHA256
  verified against UMBRA_PAYHUB_SECRET from the environment, loopback
  by default) + list + mark-sent. Payments queue locally; keys are
  minted OFFLINE - no private key, no email credentials in code.
- Code signing: scripts/release.py --sign gates on UMBRA_SIGN_CERT;
  without a certificate the build is recorded UNSIGNED in the checklist
  and SUPPORT.md documents the TODO.
- Secret hygiene: gitleaks in CI (full history), scripts/pre-commit
  hook (blocks commits containing key material), and the existing
  wheel/bundle scans.
- Legal: PRIVACY.md (local-first data inventory, GDPR/DPDP baseline)
  and REFUND.md (14-day promise, process, non-refundables), linked
  from the README and the storefront footer.
- Tests: expiry, device binding, revocation, max_devices refusal,
  trial lifecycle; update apply verify/tamper/hash-mismatch refusals +
  installer invocation; payhub HMAC valid/invalid/missing, queue,
  mark-sent.
- Version 3.19.0.
